{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1523182-running-mirrored-sabbat-camarilla-games","id":1523182,"title":"Running mirrored Sabbat/Camarilla games","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-09-26T11:15:55-07:00","id":1523182,"title":"Running mirrored Sabbat/Camarilla games","content":"My current Victorian Age Vampire game is coming to something like a stop (or at least a pause), and I'm starting to set my sights on the next game I might run. We have a large play group (8-9 players), so my mind is running to a possible mirrored game with co-STs. Run one group as a Camarilla coterie, and another as a Sabbat pack, both within the same city at the same time. Probably a siege game, to make something like that a bit more likely.<br /> <br /> Has anyone ever run anything like this before? I have little thoughts on how to plan or run a thing like this, so wondering what the possibilities and pitfalls are.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://wondrousatlas.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">Wondrous Atlas of Creation's Destiny</a> - I cohost an Exalted podcast focusing on examining the setting's lore and how to develop them into elements to use in games.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/924488-homebuilt-exalted-st-screen-reference-sheet\" target=\"_blank\">Ex3 Reference Materials</a> - currently includes an ST screen, common actions sheet, weapons reference sheet, character creation summary and mortal QCs reference sheet.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/497-xerxes","name":"Xerxes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar497_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-26T13:32:58-07:00","id":1523184,"title":"","content":"I played in a Sabbat game for awhile, where we were a single pack sent to take over a city/meet final death trying.<br /> <br /> Our interactions with the Camarilla were pretty infrequent, as we were more of a creeping infiltration into the city, slowly understanding who was who and targeting important figures.<br /> <br /> Lacking a support network meant a lot of the social elements of Vampire were absent, and the pack dynamics were a big driver of drama and conflict. Generally there was a big tension between the members of the pack who favored a slow and methodical approach and those who were willing to go wild. When people did go wild, dealing with the fallout from that was often pretty intense.<br /> <br /> In terms of game setup our storyteller used a major city nearby everyone was somewhat familiar with, then mapped out his Camarilla city and built out the entire vampire population and noted their havens and relationships with each other. Based on what you described you'd probably want to do something similar or use a published location that is well built out like Chicago.<br /> <br /> A co-gm works great, at one point I was in an Exalted game that got huge, and I dropped out to become a co-gm. Typically we alternated which group we ran so neither of us got out of touch with what was happening, and then compared notes the next day and discussed the story arc for each group and the upcoming session. Some friends have run semi-organized play games as part of local gaming organizations, where one person is 'show runner' with helper-gms who are running different tables with different sessions based on the overall arc for the season. The issue they ran into sometimes was the helper gms not having a good handle on some NPCs so players felt like NPCs were acting strangely on occasion. I'd mostly just work to make sure your partner has a good handle things, and be willing to be fluid about who runs what.<br /> <br /> For a game like this I'd probably keep it night by night to some degree, and then just keep an eye out for possible intersections. At first I'd avoid actually having PCs run into each other, and if one side might cross paths with the other just by fiat have them miss each other, and use that to give both sides clues they can pick up on (or miss). Then any crossover is something you build up to, and can plan.<br /> <br /> In terms of prep I think you need a well defined location so you can figure out how things are shifting and changing as it progresses. I know in vampire games I've run I don't always think about exactly where each NPC havens and how they protect themselves, but it is probably important to have that level of detail here. Similarly things like regular hunting grounds and such are also important because disruptions to the regular haunts of PCs and NPCs are how the Camarilla players are going to see the effects of the Sabbat incursion. On the Sabbat side, stumbling into the hunting ground of a Camarilla NPC is going to increase the likelihood they have some kind of encounter with a member of the Camarilla. That level of prep is also useful to highlight the home court advantage of the Camarilla. The Sabbat coming in are going to be pretty low information as to what things are like on the ground, while the locals will have a sense of territory, who's who, and how to leverage those things to their advantage. The Camarilla players will have the power of local knowledge, and the Sabbat have the power of incredible violence, maek sure both sides feel like their approach is a real advantage.<br /> <br /> It is also helpful to decide at which point the Camarilla starts to lend support to the city in the form of archons, ghoul hit squads, etc... Also worth thinking about who's waiting in the wings for their chance at a position of power, and will use this as a pretense to stage a coup of some kind. There should be some Ancillae who'd be happy to see an Elder or two go down, who are certain they can hold this tiger by the tail, only to realize oh no they can't.<br /> <br /> A full siege will let you keep a social element for the Sabbat players as their characters have to navigate the politics of the Sabbat, another option would be for the Pack to be infiltrating the city and masquerading as Camarilla vampires. The latter could be fun, but it would really depend on the players and how paranoid you plan on things being. In general something I felt was missing from the Sabbat game I mentioned before was a social element, either with Camarilla vampires who wanted to use the situation to their advantage, or other Sabbat. To that end a notable Anarch presence in the city might offer some fertile ground for both factions to be trying to win over hearts and minds to sway outsiders to their cause.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4270-literatzi","name":"literatzi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4270_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}